WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.860 --> 00:00:04.460
These toads are impostors,

00:00:04.460 --> 00:00:08.160
They’re the latest example of using 3D-printing

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.320
to study animal behavior.

00:00:11.060 --> 00:00:14.480
With the first rains, neotropical yellow toads

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.139
begin an intense breeding season

00:00:16.139 --> 00:00:18.870
that can last little more than a day.

00:00:18.870 --> 00:00:21.530
For this brief period, the male toads switch

00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:26.040
their normal drab brown to a vibrant lemon-yellow.

00:00:27.600 --> 00:00:29.880
This year they will be joined by a handful of

00:00:29.880 --> 00:00:33.050
impersonators, as scientists try to find out more

00:00:33.050 --> 00:00:35.300
about the toads’ mating behavior

00:00:35.300 --> 00:00:38.230
The researchers will place a female into an arena

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:41.510
with two 3D-printed male Robo-toads,

00:00:41.510 --> 00:00:43.720
one more brightly colored than the other.

00:00:43.720 --> 00:00:46.540
By recording which model the female prefers,

00:00:46.540 --> 00:00:49.390
they hope to find out how the intensity of yellow

00:00:49.390 --> 00:00:52.530
affects the female’s choice-- something previously

00:00:52.530 --> 00:00:55.860
difficult to accomplish with hand-made replicas.

00:00:57.750 --> 00:00:59.960
Researchers have also used 3D-printing to look at

00:00:59.969 --> 00:01:03.579
the mating preferences of the northern map turtle.

00:01:03.579 --> 00:01:05.439
The males are known to breed with females

00:01:05.439 --> 00:01:07.700
up to 10 times their mass.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.300
But is size something these males are looking for?

00:01:11.670 --> 00:01:14.060
Scientists printed two models of female turtles,

00:01:14.070 --> 00:01:16.040
identical except for their size,

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.640
and placed them at the bottom of a lake.

00:01:18.640 --> 00:01:20.490
The wild male turtles attempted to mate

00:01:20.490 --> 00:01:23.640
with the larger of the two females more often,

00:01:23.640 --> 00:01:26.740
suggesting that size is an important consideration

00:01:26.740 --> 00:01:28.260
for these turtles.

00:01:29.670 --> 00:01:32.580
Ornithologists have used 3D printed eggs--

00:01:32.590 --> 00:01:34.990
to study how robins deal with eggs from

00:01:34.990 --> 00:01:39.340
parasitic cowbirds that are laid in their nests.

00:01:40.740 --> 00:01:43.850
With 3D printing, researchers can create cowbird eggs

00:01:43.850 --> 00:01:45.590
that vary subtly in size and shape--

00:01:45.590 --> 00:01:48.729
and their research can now be reproduced

00:01:48.729 --> 00:01:51.980
by others using the same 3D model.

00:01:52.720 --> 00:01:54.250
Once any of these models have been created,

00:01:54.250 --> 00:01:57.000
it can easily be mass-produced.

00:01:57.000 --> 00:01:58.100
Despite these advantages --

00:01:58.100 --> 00:02:02.159
3D-printed animals are still rare in research.

00:02:02.160 --> 00:02:05.460
But as 3D printers get more common and less complex

00:02:06.040 --> 00:02:08.100
you might want to keep an eye out

00:02:08.100 --> 00:02:10.100
for wildlife imposters.

